3d printing is a process that uses  high-tech machinery to turn a digital  file into a physical object most often  it's associated with industrial items  like automobile parts or medical  implants but one company in california  has found a whole new use for the  technology one where the d in 3d might  just as well stand for delicious our  jamie wax has the story  in the modern world we hear the term 3d  printing so frequently that it's almost  become meaningless  but at a tech slash culinary startup in  downtown los angeles 3d printing is  creating something very specific and  sweet a lot of these designs yeah like i  we have inspiration from chefs or just  we kind of have some crazy ideas in the  lab and we're just like okay let's just  make this  really fun food product sugar lab  creates small beautiful detailed  sculptures every one of them edible  creative director william who showed us  some highlights including some  custom-made designs special for our  visit like these cake toppers tiny bites  of a latte filled with ganache that was  a very complex bite i mean i've got  chocolate i've got peppermint and i've  got coffee flavor in here  but they're all sort of hitting at a  slightly different time and in a  different way yeah we love that kind of  bringing our you know customers through  that experience craft beer bon bons  incorporating various beers into the  filling the visual printing on these is  incredible we kind of took inspiration  from breweries to make really poppy  labels and then yeah with the shadows we  wanted to really emphasize the that  little pop top on top and they even add  an element of fun and sparkle to drinks  with these glitter bombs these are lime  flavored printed with edible glitter as  you can see it's melting there with the  glitter i mean that's just fun yeah  to watch much less drink that's just fun  well cheers to your creativity cheers  thank you like many revolutionary ideas  the whole thing started almost by  accident in the college department of  founder kyle von hassell who was  studying architecture i basically was  grocery shopping with my  wife and we brought home some  cake batter like in a box and we get  into our tiny grad student apartment and  we realized we didn't have an oven so  there was no way to bake it actually  and  we  we thought what would it be like to  actually use this 3d printer that we've  been using for architectural purposes  and pour in sugar could you 3d print  frosting could it could you make it  sculptural could you add color after  some experimenting his vision eventually  grew beyond the existing capability we  realized that the technology couldn't go  much farther unless we did a lot of  research a lot of homework and developed  a 3d printer from scratch which would  require a lot of innovation  a lot of invention that's when he  brought in co-founder and chief  operating officer megan bozeman  3d printing was the focus of your work  and your life at the time when you heard  about this application what did you  first think it's a market that had not  even been tapped into and the technology  is something that could deliver a whole  new product in a whole new way allow  chefs to be creative in a whole new way  to see that firsthand i donned a hair  net required around the equipment and  toured the sugar lab itself so victoria  this seems like an incredibly fun job in  the culinary world it absolutely is  easily the most interesting and  different thing i've ever done in-house  chef victoria johnson walked me through  the process of printing  some of sugar lab's delectable creations  starting with the computer design  i am  re-configuring these strawberries then  to where the magic happens at one of  their 3d printers i'll show you so  we got to get wow all this powder out of  here now i know where these are right  but i will show you you've done this a  lot so we've got one here  and uh i mean that that's it that's the  whole thing but of course you can see  it's covered in powder so we always give  it a little tap to get all the powder  out of the inside  that is that's truly surprising i have  to say it's completely wrong  the color the texture and everything  just rise up  that's amazing with the right blend of  business creativity and technology  sugar lab got the attention of the  culinary community how important was it  to involve chefs in this process uh it's  been critical the people that we asked  were pastry chefs they were our first  clients nobody  would want to buy these if they were not  delicious  and so the chefs really help us bridge  that from beautiful that's what the  architects really deliver to beautiful  and delicious and an experience that  people have not had before and  collaborations with chefs have led to  innovative and even savory uses of the  technology like top chef winner mae  lynn's 3d printed quail egg and las  vegas french chef yuri charcevsky's  hyper realistic honeycomb they see  something that they've never eaten  before and then they're surprised with  this  the filling that he's put in their goat  cheese mixed with other  ingredients that he then bakes and  serves and it's an experiential  meal for the customer the instagramable  nature of sugar lab sculptures have led  to collaborations with celebrities like  kylie jenner but kyle von hasslen still  sees a bigger future for the dream that  started in that college apartment where  do you want this to go i'd love for this  to expand across the country  into other countries an increasingly  urbanized united states where food  scarcity issues could come up being able  to have those local ingredients and  print with local 3d printers i think  could make a really big difference and  we want to make sure the technology is  out there and distributed wildly enough  that it can have that kind of impact for  cbs saturday morning jamie wax  los angeles  here we go take these diamond artists  it's like a little diamond glitter bomb  